{"qid":"Q49326","names":["Majapahit"],"title":"Majapahit","endonym":null,"capsule":"Majapahit was a Hindu-Buddhist maritime empire based in eastern Java, founded c. 1293 after Raden Wijaya repelled a Mongol naval expedition and established the capital near modern Trowulan. Under Hayam Wuruk (r. 1350–1389) and his prime minister Gajah Mada, the empire claimed suzerainty over most of the Indonesian archipelago, the Malay Peninsula, and parts of Borneo and the Philippines, as attested in the Nagarakretagama poem of 1365. After Gajah Mada's death and Hayam Wuruk's succession crises, the empire fragmented under pressure from the rising Islamic states of Demak and Malacca; the last court relocated to Bali around 1527.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The empire's core territory in eastern Java is confirmed by extensive archaeological and epigraphic evidence. Outer tributary claims, stretching across the archipelago, rest primarily on the Nagarakretagama panegyric and are widely considered exaggerated; territorial polygons for distant regions are highly conjectural.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Majapahit empire","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Majapahit-empire"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Majapahit","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Majapahit"}],"britannica":"place/Majapahit-empire","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Majapahit","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Kertarajasa Jayawardhana (Raden Wijaya)","nativeName":"ꦏꦼꦂꦠꦫꦗꦱꦗꦪꦮꦂꦣꦤ","roman":"Kertarajasa Jayawardhana","start":1293,"end":1309,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Jayanagara","nativeName":"ꦗꦪꦤꦴꦒꦫ","roman"